.pa
.ce
Introduction
     Structured programming is a collection of 
techniques for writing programs that are correct,
modifiable, readable and comprehensible all with
a reasonable amount of effort.  While the
proponents of structured programming claim that
their techniques are general for programming
of all problems (at least those that are understandable),
it is not clear that they apply to AI programming.
The background of those in structured programming is
usually systems programming and the question arises as
to whether what they claim works for operating systems
works for intelligent systems.  This paper will
discuss this issue.
     There are many different techniques associated with
structured programming and it is likely that some methods
are general to all programming and some only to problems
with a particular structure.  To decide what structured
programming has to say to AI and related fields we shall
go through the techniques one by one.
.sp
.ce
Proofs of Correctness
     Structured programming includes techniques for proving
that a program is correct, that is it satisfies its
input/output specifications.  Most of the literature deals
with techniques of specifying pre- and post-conditions and
invariants in a first order logic and then proving correctness.
The question that arises when one considers AI problems is
whether the specifications can be expressed in first order
logic or any logic at all.  Consider SHRDLU, a very
famous AI program written by Terry Winograd considered by many to be a great breakthrough in the problem
of teaching machines to understand English.  But what could
be its specification?  One goal of the program is to create
a system that can understand a subset of English as well as
humans do. Surely such a goal can not be represented as
a logical proposition to be proved.  Even the input can not
be well defined, that is it can not be logically specified
as being a subset of English. 
     However, it could be argued that although the system as
a whole can not be shown to be correct, many of the sub-modules
can. Perhaps it could be proved that the read routine will
read until a period, question mark, or exclamation mark and
return each word as they are typed.  Perhaps it could
be proved that the routine that clears the top of a block does
indeed clear off all those blocks that are above that block.
But could it be proved that the syntactic parser will return
a "correct" parse of the sentence?  The question then arises
is it much use to prove some of the subsystems correct when
all that really matters is that the entire system performs as
it should.  It could be argued that this would reduce the 
number of bugs in the entire system by removing them from many
of the subsystems.  However, at the present state of the art,
the effort involved in proving the straight forward subsystems correct
is probably greater than the benefits.  Winograd wanted to
work on understanding English not read routines.  Generally
it is the uninteresting "system" parts of an AI program that
can be proved by the available structured programming 
techniques.  The interesting problems in AI are usually those
that are not well defined (in the sense of McCarthy), that is
there is not algorithm that can be applied to check if the
output is correct.  The interesting solutions to AI problems
are those that work most of the time 
but sometimes fail, as with people.
An AI chess program has a high chess rating or wins often 
but does not win
every game.
.sp
.ce
Hierarchy Versus Heterarchy
    To be structured, among other things, a program must be
hierarchical, that is no two modules may call each other.  For
example, in systems design it is argued that the code supporting
processes and the code for supporting files should have only
a one way dependence.  That is the file system may use
processes but if it does then processes may not use the file
system (and vise versa).  The rational is that this greatly
eases the tasks of modifying, verifying, and understanding
the system.  However, heterarchy is a popular concept in AI.
Many claim that the reason that SHRDLU succeeded where
earlier programs failed was that it was heterarchical.
Previously most attempts to understand English were
hierarchical, that is they allowed the semantic module to
call on the syntactic module but there were few links the
other way.  Winograd believed that to parse the sentence
efficiently (and at all in some cases) the semantic module
and the inference module
must be used.  So his system became heterarchical, worked,
but suffered much of what proponents of structured programming
would expect---it is very difficult to modify or understand.
     This use of heterarchy is not limited to language
understanding systems but has been a powerful technique in
vision systems.  In trying to recognize a scene it was
found that the local knowledge interacts heavily with the
global knowledge. So programs were written in which the
line and vertex finders call upon the global scene recognizer
for help and of course the global module calls upon the local
modules for information. It would seem from these examples that
there are situations that inherently require heterarchy and
even though it leads to unstructured programs it sometimes is
the best way to solve the problem.
     However, there is a middle ground that can be taken.
That would be to create a higher level "control" module
and have the potentially heterarchical routines interact only
by going through the higher level module.  A good example
of this would be the speech understanding system under 
development at BBN by Woods and others.  There are
six potentially heterarchical modules, the phonetic module,
the lexicographical module, the syntactic module, the
semantic module, the pragmatic module and a bookkeeping
module.  For example, the lexicographical module usually can't on its
own decide what the beginning and end of words
should be.  Sometimes the syntactic module
will suggest looking for a short word because it expects a
preposition.  Sometimes the pragmatic module will suggest a
word because it seems the most likely to be next in this
context. (For example, in listening to one talk about
structured programming one would expect the word spoken
after the word "structured" to most likely be "programming").
Yet these modules and the the syntactic and phonetic modules
need to get information from the lexicographical module. But
Woods system in not exactly heterarchical since all these
modules return in addition to answers to calls, requests for
more information from another module which the control module
calls and then calls the asking routine with the answer.
Such a system seems more understandable, more modifiable,
than a strictly heterarchical system and less so than a
hierarchical system that would obviously fail in speech
recognition.  Notice that this does not necessarily imply
that the use of heterarchy is because the problem is not
"well understood", it is more likely just the result of the
structure of
some problems.
.sp
.ce
Control Structures
     Structured programming suggests that control structures
should be limited to a small set usually consisting of only
concatenation, "if---then---else---" conditionals, case
statements, simple loops, and structured subroutine or function
calls.  This is sufficient to be theoretically complete
so only the questions of ease of use and perversion of use
remain.  By perversion of use, it is meant that one constructs
very unstructured control structures out of the allowed ones.
Theoretically it should be possible to implement gotos using
only the structured control structures.  In other words, in
writing AI programs is there a need for other control
structures that would cause a programmer to violate the
spirit of the restriction to only structured control structures?
     The development of recent AI languages would seems to
indicate that other control structures are needed.  In 
PLANNER, CONNIVER and ACTORS strong
emphasis is placed on pattern-directed invocation of
procedures.  This delegation of control to local components
has uses that vary from maintaining a relatively consistent
and complete data base, to putting forth hypothesises,
to protection from assumptions (by adding demons that watch
for violations of assumptions).
The effects of putting knowledge in autonomous routines
that are not directly controlled by the program are many.
Modifiability and modularity (and thus understandability)
are greatly increase since demons are usually indexed by what they
can do and thus new ones can be added or old ones
modified without requiring changes in any other part of
the program.  However, proofs of correctness become much
more difficult with demons sitting around waiting to take
control.  Demons can be viewed as generalized software
interrupts.  However, interrupts are a dirty word in structured
programming according to Dijkstra <2>.  But his objections
are based on problems of timing and the resulting
difficulties in complete testing.  But demons have no
timing problems and it is not clear if testing would
become any easier if the knowledge embedded in the demons
were incorporated in a structured program.
     Again there is a middle ground on this issue.
Demons need not grab control away when their triggers
are satisfied but might simply send suggestions up to
their superiors who can follow them or not as it sees
fit.  One fairly structured way of handling this would
be to ask for suggestions from demons after adding to,
removing from,
or interrogation of the data base.  The interrupt aspect
of demons is thus removed yet the modularity is preserved.
But when you know that a particular demon's suggestions
would always be followed then why not make it automatic and
simplify the higher level modules. Whichever implementation
is preferred it seems that pattern directed invocation
is very useful in AI and perhaps more structured than
conventional control structures.
     AI languages such as CONNIVER have other
unconventional control structures that may be useful.  
For instance, in CONNIVER, it is possible to
decide that perhaps this path of search or investigation
is wrong and another should be tried.  However, if the
new path fails one may want to try some more of the old
path where it was left off.  For example, an integration
program may try to use integration by parts but after
not getting too far it may try to do it some trigonometric
substitutions and this might succeed.  However, if it
doesn't we may want to try integration by parts again
but don't want to start over again.  This ability
to hang routines is often very important yet is not
structured necessarily, since anybody even a clock
interrupt may hang routines and someone else may resume
the routine. User controlled backtracking also hinges
on this control structure.  The problems of proofs of
correctness and understandably are difficult to assess
since parallelism is not very well understood.  Perhaps
if its not well understood it shouldn't be used but
it seems very useful in many situations.
     There are other new control structures developing
in the AI community.  Carl Hewitt's ACTORS
formalism is based on the notion of generalized 
uni___-directional message passing <3>.  While this
clearly violates structured programming's definition
of structured control structures there are good claims
that systems written in this language will be very
modular, modifiable, understandable (once one becomes
used to a very different way of looking at computation),
and verifiable.  It seems to have the same goals of
structured programming and yet is a very different
approach which seems not to conflict with AI
programming (which I have been attempting to show
that conventional structured programming tenets sometimes do).
There other issues of efficiency of ACTORS
versus a conventional programming but they are beyond
the scope of this paper.
.sp
.ce
Stepwise Refinement Versus the Debugging Paradigm
     Structured programming contains techniques for
designing systems top-down by recognizing various levels
of abstraction and making one design decision at a time.
By being explicit about all design decisions and allowing
only a minimum of interface assumptions between modules the hope is
that essentially correct code will be produced from the
start.  A few system development project seem to
substantiate this claim.  One of AI's powerful
ideas (after Papert) is linerization.
The slogan is, "try a first order theory 
first and then debug the mistakes".  A paradigm example
of this would be Sussman's HACKER <4> where the
system writes blocks world programs by ignoring interactions
and second order effects and then using debugging knowledge
to patch the problems.  It should be noted that if it 
has seen a similar problem before it will remember previously
debugged solutions and try those.  In other words, if the
problem is familiar step-wise refinement may work but for
poorly understood problems approximation followed by debugging
seems more successful.  This is probably a good model of
problem solving in general not just programming.  
     Once more there is a middle ground on this controversy.
Step-wise refinement and top-down design are good for
both well understood problems and the second pass for previously
poorly understood problems that have been "solved" by debugging.
The patched up program is thrown away to be replaced by a 
cleaner structured program.  But debugging has an important
role in learning (which may continue after the program is 
"finished" if it is to be continuously improved). Also
from the point of view of automatic programming the distinction
lessens since the machine can either run and patch bugs or
look ahead by simulating and planning first and then producing
the code.  Yet a plan is code itself at a higher level of
abstraction.
.sp
.ce
How AI Could Help Extend Structured Programming
     The top level goal of structured programming is
to produce reliable modifiable programs. Goals such as
proofs of correctness and understandability are subsidiary
to this top goal.  Recent work in AI and automatic
programming on self-understanding programs capable of
explaining what they do, how they do, and how to modify
what they do should help produce reliable modifiable
programs.  Machine readable and executable comments
and intentions should obviously help a programmer (whether
human or not) to more easily modify code. This built
in understanding should also increase reliability by
having the system explain what it is doing and why when asked. 
Reliability should mean that the code satisfies what the
designers think it should do.  A medical diagnosis program
isn't reliable simply because someone wrote down the
algorithm or strategies and proved that the program
follows them but because it does good diagnosis (at least
as good as a doctor).  But the doctor does more than
good diagnosis to be considered reliable, he or she
explains his or her reasoning for the diagnosis. Perhaps
trustworthy is a better word than reliable yet it is
what the user wants, not a proof of correctness, just
simple English explanations and justifications.
     When one does AI or knowledge based programming
the data structures often become very complex.  The line
between code and data fades since data is inspected, 
suggestions are returned and sometimes the data is
"executed". Especially in automatic programming where
the data that the program massages is itself a program to
be tested, proved correct, and run.  In putting medical
knowledge into the computer <5>, the data, or frames in Minsky's
sense <6> are just as hard as the program to design and
be sure that it is reliable.  Surely some of structured
programming's techniques apply readily to complex data
structures but it seems that there are special problems
here. In Martin's OWL language <7> ,for example, procedures are 
sometimes just looked at, sometimes partially executed,
sometimes executed, and sometimes compiled.  The execution
or compliation is often dependent on global information.
Does proof of correctness or step-wise refinement make
sense for such a language?  Perhaps if the methodology
of the field of structured programming were applied to
this problem it might help.
.pa
.ce
Conclusions
     The interactions between AI and structured programming
are many and varied.  In doing AI programming the 
structured programming style
of programming helps in some cases and
hinders in others.  An AI program with restricted use of gotos
and global variables and structured as hierarchically as
feasible which avoids hairy control structures when ever 
possible will be more effective, reliable and modifiable.
The program will probably be more comprehensible which
is very important for AI researchers since they are
usually more interested in understanding their programs
than having them used.  In addition to the advice
structured programming has to AI researchers similarly
AI suggests some new avenues of research for structured
programming researchers.
.pa
.sp
.ce
